# Provenance for the files in this directory (mirrors dbarts's convention).
#
# role:
#   current - the baseline the C2/C3 distributional-equivalence gates
#             compare the WALNUTS fits against.
#   superseded - a former current, kept for the historical arc. Compare
#             against the current row instead, and read its notes first:
#             a superseded perf row may have been measured with different
#             harness settings, which makes some of its rows incomparable.
#   warmup-c0 - the walnuts-warmup C0 measurement baseline (bench-warmup.R):
#             per-iter sampling wall, ESS/sec, and mean leapfrog per transition
#             across the warmup grid; the ESS/sec floor and knee C1/C2 condition on.
#
# Every per-iteration figure recorded BEFORE 412df11 came from differencing two
# COLD subprocesses, which does not measure per-iteration cost: a cold process
# pays page-in on the sample-storage arrays, that cost scales with iter, and so
# it lands on the long run and reads back as compute. The error is NOT a
# constant offset - it grows with the per-draw storage footprint, so it does
# not cancel when those rows are differenced against each other:
#     174b369  recorded 0.5/0.5/0.6 ms,  warm truth 163/136/147 us  (3-4x high)
#     3e2036b  recorded 0.137/0.122/0.108 ms, warm truth 144/133/111 us (5-8% LOW)
# That gradient, not any speedup, is the entire "4x per-iteration win" between
# them; per-iteration cost is flat across that range. So do not diff the
# per-iteration rows of any two pre-412df11 recordings. The Stan-era row is
# the one place the bias is negligible: at 8-16 ms/iter an absolute error of a
# few hundred us is 2-4%. bench-perf.R differences in one warm subprocess as of
# 412df11. This never applied to the bench-warmup row: bench-warmup.R has
# always differenced its two fits in-process.
#
# file                                 role     commit   build state                                    machine/arch   contents
